Abstract

This dissertation develops a complete formal theory for the static compilation of pipeline‑parallel deep learning workloads, from single‑iteration training to infinite online reinforcement learning loops. We introduce the Bare‑Graph Intermediate Representation (BG‑IR), a timed, resource‑aware dataflow graph that captures computation, communication, memory, and gradient flow in a unified static model. For training alone we design three algorithms—Stencil‑AD (static reverse‑mode differentiation), TopoMem (provably minimal memory planning), and the Asynchronous Overlapped Pipeline (AOP, the unique zero‑bubble schedule)—and prove a Unified Optimality Theorem: the combination simultaneously achieves exact gradients, zero bubble, and minimal peak memory. We then extend the theory to online reinforcement learning by embedding the training loop inside a Cyclo‑Static Dataflow (CSDF) graph, guaranteeing decidability of liveness and boundedness. Within this extended model we define the Co‑Scheduling Problem, prove its NP‑hardness via a reduction from preemptive single‑machine scheduling with release times and deadlines, and present three new algorithms: static batching with deadline guarantees, joint KV‑cache and activation memory planning (Co‑Mem), and AOP‑RL, a zero‑bubble schedule that fills pipeline bubbles with inference tokens. A Unified Co‑Optimality Theorem asserts that under mild assumptions the static schedule simultaneously maximises training throughput, satisfies all inference deadlines, and minimises peak memory. The entire theory is designed for direct realisation in a C/C++ compiler that generates a single, statically scheduled binary. The work provides the first comprehensive mathematical foundation for fully static AI infrastructure on exascale GPU clusters.

