Chapter 1: Introduction

Modern deep learning training suffers from a widening gap between theoretical hardware peak and realised throughput. At the scale of 220 000 next‑generation accelerators (GB300 superchips with 800 Gbps interconnects), dynamic runtime overheads—Python hosts, just‑in‑time autograd, heuristic communication libraries—can waste more than half of the available compute. Moreover, the most advanced training workflows are no longer isolated; they interleave high‑throughput inference (rollout generation) with training in online reinforcement learning loops. All existing systems for these workloads are dynamic, making decisions at runtime without formal guarantees.

This dissertation presents a unified formal framework that rethinks both problems from first principles. We ask: can the entire training iteration, and even the full training–inference loop, be specified as a static computation and scheduled provably optimally at compile time? We answer affirmatively by constructing a complete chain of mathematical models and algorithms, each with rigorous proofs of correctness, optimality, and complexity.

The thesis is built in two parts. Part I treats the pure training iteration. We define BG‑IR, a static graph model that captures compute, communication, memory allocation, and gradient dependencies. We then prove that optimally scheduling a BG‑IR under memory limits is NP‑hard, which motivates restricting to a structured family of pipeline schedules. Within this family we design Stencil‑AD (a compile‑time graph‑rewriting system for reverse‑mode differentiation), TopoMem (an exact memory planner that achieves minimal peak memory for the chosen schedule), and AOP (a zero‑bubble pipeline schedule that is unique and makespan‑optimal). The capstone is a Unified Optimality Theorem showing that the three algorithms together achieve exact gradients, zero bubble, and memory optimality.

Part II extends the model to online reinforcement learning. Training and inference are interleaved on the same GPU cluster. Naively adding cycles to BG‑IR yields a Kahn process network where liveness is undecidable, so we restrict to the Cyclo‑Static Dataflow (CSDF) model, which guarantees polynomial‑time schedulability. We define the Co‑Scheduling Problem, prove its NP‑hardness by reduction from classical scheduling theory, and then develop static batching, Co‑Mem (joint KV‑cache and activation memory planning), and AOP‑RL (a zero‑bubble co‑schedule that fills pipeline bubbles with inference tokens). A second Unified Co‑Optimality Theorem provides formal guarantees for the full RL loop.

All algorithms are designed to be directly translatable into a C/C++ compiler that emits a monolithic, statically scheduled binary—no Python, no dynamic dispatch, no garbage collection.

