Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Notation

Let \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} denote non‑negative real numbers, \mathbb{N} the natural numbers \{0,1,2,\dots\}, and \mathbb{Z} the integers. For a finite set S, |S| is its cardinality. A graph G=(V,E) is a pair of finite sets with E\subseteq V\times V. In a directed graph, (u,v) denotes an edge from u to v. A directed acyclic graph (DAG) has no directed cycles.

2.2 Scheduling and Resource Constraints

A scheduling problem consists of a set of jobs J with processing times p_j\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}, a set of machines, and additional constraints (precedence, resource limits, release times, deadlines). A schedule assigns a start time s_j\ge 0 to each job such that no machine executes two jobs simultaneously and all constraints are respected. The makespan is \max_j (s_j+p_j).

A classic NP‑hard problem is 1|r_j, d_j, pmtn|C_{\max}: preemptive single‑machine scheduling with release times r_j and deadlines d_j, minimising makespan (Du and Leung, 1993; Chen and Yuan, 2021). It is NP‑hard in the strong sense.

2.3 Cyclo‑Static Dataflow (CSDF)

A CSDF graph (Bilsen et al., 1996) generalises synchronous dataflow. Each actor v has an integer period \varphi(v) and, for each output edge e, a sequence of token production counts p_{v,e}(0),\dots,p_{v,e}(\varphi(v)-1); similarly, for each input edge, a sequence of consumption counts c_{v,e}(0),\dots,c_{v,e}(\varphi(v)-1). Execution is periodic: in phase k \bmod \varphi(v) the actor fires, consuming and producing the prescribed numbers of tokens. A CSDF graph is consistent if there exists a positive integer repetition vector q(v) such that for every edge (u,v),

q(u)\sum_{i=0}^{\varphi(u)-1} p_{u,e}(i) = q(v)\sum_{j=0}^{\varphi(v)-1} c_{v,e}(j).

Consistency is necessary and sufficient for the existence of a live, bounded periodic schedule. Liveness and schedule construction can be decided in polynomial time (Marchetti and Munier-Kordon, 2009).

