Chapter 3: BG‑IR — A Formal Model of Pipeline Training

We define a static intermediate representation that captures the entire training iteration—compute, communication, memory—as a single directed graph.

3.1 Definition of BG‑IR

Definition 3.1 (BG‑IR Graph).
A BG‑IR is an 8‑tuple G = (V, E, \tau, \delta, d, \kappa_V, \kappa_E, \sigma) where:

· V is a finite set of nodes.
· d: V \to \{1,\dots,P\} assigns each node to a pipeline stage (device).
· \kappa_V: V \to \{\texttt{compute}, \texttt{send}, \texttt{recv}, \texttt{alloc}, \texttt{free}\} is the node kind.
· \tau: V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is the duration for compute nodes, or the data volume (bytes) for send/recv nodes; for alloc/free, \tau=0.
· \sigma: V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is the memory size of the allocated tensor (alloc nodes only).
· E \subseteq V\times V is a set of directed edges.
· \kappa_E: E \to \{\texttt{data}, \texttt{gradient}, \texttt{memory}, \texttt{comm\_order}\} is the edge kind.
· \delta: E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} assigns a minimum latency to each edge.

A forward BG‑IR contains only data and memory edges, no gradient or comm_order edges, and is acyclic.

3.2 Schedule and Validity

Definition 3.2 (Schedule). A schedule for G is a function S: V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} assigning a start time to every node.

A schedule is valid if it satisfies:

1. Precedence: \forall (u,v)\in E,\; S(v) \ge S(u) + \tau(u) + \delta(u,v).
2. Device mutual exclusion: For any distinct u,v on the same stage, [S(u), S(u)+\tau(u)) \cap [S(v), S(v)+\tau(v)) = \emptyset.
3. Link buffer capacity: For each adjacent stage pair (p,p+1) with buffer size B_{\text{link}}(p) (bytes), at any time t the total volume of messages in flight on that link does not exceed B_{\text{link}}(p).
4. Device memory capacity: At any time t, the total size of live tensors on stage p does not exceed B_{\text{mem}}(p). A tensor is live from its alloc start to its free start.

The makespan is M(S) = \max_{v\in V} (S(v)+\tau(v)).

3.3 Complexity of Optimal Scheduling

Problem 3.1 (PIPELINE‑SCHED).
Given a BG‑IR G, pipeline depth P, buffer and memory capacities, and a deadline T, does there exist a valid schedule with makespan \le T?

Theorem 3.1. PIPELINE‑SCHED is NP‑hard even for P=1.

Proof. Reduction from Resource‑Constrained Project Scheduling (RCPSP) with a single renewable resource (memory) and a single machine. Given an RCPSP instance with jobs j=1,\dots,n, processing times p_j, memory demands r_j, precedence constraints \prec, memory capacity R, and machine capacity 1, construct a single‑stage BG‑IR:

· For each job j, create nodes \texttt{alloc}_j (\sigma=r_j,\tau=0), \texttt{compute}_j (\tau=p_j), \texttt{free}_j (\tau=0).
· Edges: \texttt{alloc}_j \to \texttt{compute}_j \to \texttt{free}_j.
· For each precedence j\prec k, add edge \texttt{free}_j \to \texttt{alloc}_k with \delta=0.
· Set B_{\text{mem}}(1)=R, deadline T = D.

A valid BG‑IR schedule with makespan \le T exists iff the RCPSP instance is feasible. Since RCPSP is NP‑hard, PIPELINE‑SCHED is NP‑hard. ∎

This intractability justifies focusing on a structured family of schedules, which we develop in Chapter 6.

