Chapter 4: Stencil‑AD — Static Reverse‑Mode Differentiation

4.1 Graph Transformation

Stencil‑AD transforms a forward BG‑IR G_{\text{fwd}} into a complete forward‑backward graph G_{\text{full}} that statically encodes reverse‑mode automatic differentiation, including cross‑stage gradient communication and optional recomputation.

For every forward compute node u producing tensor a, a new backward compute node \bar{u} is created on the same stage, with duration \tau_{\text{VJP}}(u). Gradient edges are added from \bar{v} to \bar{u} for each data edge u\to v. Cross‑stage data paths u \to \texttt{send} \to \texttt{recv} \to v generate gradient communication: a send\_grad node on the consumer stage and a recv\_grad node on the producer stage, with appropriate edges and durations. The loss node’s backward node receives a constant gradient of 1.

Recomputation substitution: if an activation is marked for recomputation, a copy of its producer node is inserted before the first backward consumer, and the original storage edge is replaced.

4.2 Correctness

Theorem 4.1 (Gradient correctness).
Let G_{\text{full}} = \texttt{Stencil‑AD}(G_{\text{fwd}}) and let S be any valid schedule for G_{\text{full}} that evaluates forward nodes in topological order. Then upon completion, the tensors at the backward outputs of parameter nodes equal the true gradients \nabla_\theta L.

Proof. By induction on the topological depth of forward nodes. The loss node’s adjoint is correctly initialised to 1. Assume all nodes of depth >k have correct adjoints. A node u of depth k gathers gradient contributions from all consumers (which have correctly computed their VJPs and transmitted gradients). The VJP of f_u then computes \partial L / \partial(\text{input of } u), matching the chain rule. Recomputation uses identical inputs and deterministic operations, so it yields the same forward value. ∎

4.3 Complexity and Deadlock‑Freedom

The transformation runs in time linear in the size of G_{\text{fwd}}. The resulting graph is a DAG because backward edges go from higher depth to lower depth; thus any topological schedule is deadlock‑free under the buffer assumptions of Chapter 3.

