Chapter 5: Pipeline Memory Minimisation (TopoMem)

5.1 The PMM Problem

Given a fixed schedule S (e.g., from AOP), an activation tensor a has a production time t_{\text{prod}}(a) and last‑use time t_{\text{last}}(a). A memory plan may store it continuously, offload it to slow memory and reload it, or recompute it. Offloading consumes transfer time 2 s_a / b from the device’s idle intervals; the tensor occupies fast memory only during transfers. The goal is to minimise peak fast‑memory usage.

Problem 5.1 (PMM). Given a BG‑IR with a fixed schedule and idle intervals, find a feasible memory plan minimising peak memory.

Theorem 5.1. PMM is NP‑hard in general.

Proof. PMM contains the Dynamic Storage Allocation (DSA) problem as a special case: a single device, all activations produced at time 0 and last‑used at time T; DMA is the single resource. The problem of placing offload/reload intervals to minimise peak memory is exactly DSA, which is strongly NP‑hard (Garey and Johnson, 1979). ∎

5.2 TopoMem for Proper Intervals

In the AOP schedule (Chapter 6), the activation live intervals on each stage satisfy the proper interval property: if t_{\text{prod}}(a) < t_{\text{prod}}(b) then t_{\text{last}}(a) \le t_{\text{last}}(b); i.e., they are nested, non‑crossing.

Theorem 5.2 (Exact TopoMem). For a schedule with proper intervals and given idle time budget, the greedy algorithm that offloads the largest tensors first yields an optimal memory plan.

Proof. The memory profile at any time is the sum of sizes of live tensors covering that time. Offloading a tensor removes its size from the profile during its entire lifetime except transfer windows. Because intervals are proper, offloading a larger tensor always reduces the peak by at least as much as offloading a smaller one covering the same peak, with no greater idle‑time cost. A simple exchange argument shows that any optimal plan can be transformed into the greedy one without increasing peak memory. ∎

TopoMem thus computes the minimum peak memory in polynomial time for the AOP schedule.

