Chapter 6: The AOP Schedule — Zero‑Bubble Pipeline Parallelism

6.1 Model and Assumptions

We consider a linear pipeline of P identical stages, training with M micro‑batches. Each micro‑batch undergoes three operations per stage: forward (F), backward‑input (B_I), backward‑weight (B_W). All operations have equal duration \tau; communication is overlapped via separate DMA engines and a credit‑based buffer system with capacity at least 2 micro‑batch activations.

6.2 AOP Algorithm

AOP assigns start times using a priority‑driven state machine. Initially each stage may start at time s-1 (earliest start). At each step, a stage selects the highest‑priority ready operation: B_I > F > B_W, choosing the smallest micro‑batch index among ties. Buffer credits are consumed upon send and returned upon the corresponding receive. The algorithm never leaves a stage idle when M \ge 2P-1.

6.3 Zero Bubble and Optimality

Theorem 6.1 (Zero Bubble). For M \ge 2P-1 and buffer credits \ge 2, AOP achieves zero bubble: every stage s is busy from time s-1 to s-1+3M, with makespan 3M + P - 1.

Proof. By induction on the global time steps, we verify an invariant: the number of completed operations matches the schedule produced by the priority rule, and the buffer credits never drop below zero. The condition M\ge 2P-1 guarantees that after the initial P-1 steps, a steady state is reached where every stage has a continuous supply of ready operations. The priority rule ensures that backward‑input operations fill the gaps that would otherwise become bubbles. ∎

Theorem 6.2 (Uniqueness). Any zero‑bubble schedule for the same model must coincide with AOP up to reordering of independent B_W operations.

Proof. Any deviation would either violate a dependency or cause a stage to idle at some point, contradicting zero bubble. An exchange argument shows that the priority order is forced. ∎

Theorem 6.3 (Makespan Optimality). The makespan of AOP, 3M+P-1, is the minimum possible for any valid schedule.

Proof. Stage P cannot start before time P-1 (forward wave latency) and must execute exactly 3M operations; hence makespan \ge P-1+3M. AOP attains this bound. ∎

