Chapter 8: BG‑IR‑RL — A Cyclic Model for Online RL

8.1 Motivation and CSDF Restriction

An online RL loop is inherently cyclic: the model generates tokens (inference), observes rewards, updates weights (training), and repeats. Naively extending BG‑IR with back‑edges yields a Kahn process network, where liveness is undecidable (Parks, 1995). We therefore restrict cycles to the Cyclo‑Static Dataflow (CSDF) model, which permits polynomial‑time liveness and schedulability analysis.

8.2 Formal Definition

Definition 8.1 (BG‑IR‑RL). A BG‑IR‑RL is a 9‑tuple (V,E,\tau,\delta,d,\kappa_V,\kappa_E,\sigma,\pi), where the first eight components are as in BG‑IR, and \pi: V\to \{\texttt{train},\texttt{infer},\texttt{env}\} partitions nodes into training, inference, and environment subgraphs.

· The training subgraph is a DAG (as in Part I).
· The inference subgraph is a CSDF graph with period \varphi = L (the episode token length). In phase i, it produces one token per active episode.
· The environment subgraph is a single CSDF actor with fixed latency T_{\text{env}}.

The graph is consistent if a positive integer repetition vector q exists satisfying the balance equations.

Lemma 8.1. The BG‑IR‑RL constructed from a round with E episodes of length L and M_{\text{train}} micro‑batches is consistent, with q=1 for training, q=L for inference token nodes, and q=1 for the environment.

Proof. The environment produces one state per episode; each inference token node fires L times per episode, consuming that state and producing tokens; the training node consumes exactly E\cdot L tokens per round and fires once per round. The balance equations hold with the given repetition vector. ∎

Consistency guarantees the existence of a live, bounded periodic schedule (Bilsen et al., 1996).

8.3 The Co‑Scheduling Problem

Problem 8.1 (CO‑SCHED). Given a consistent BG‑IR‑RL, inference deadlines D_e, and a P-stage linear pipeline, find a valid periodic static schedule that maximises the number of training micro‑batches per round while satisfying all inference deadlines.

Theorem 8.2. CO‑SCHED is NP‑hard.

Proof. Reduction from 1|r_j, d_j, pmtn | C_{\max}. Construct a single‑stage (P=1) BG‑IR‑RL. Each job j becomes a training node of duration p_j, preceded by an inference node of duration \varepsilon with release time r_j and deadline d_j. The inference node’s deadline enforces the job’s completion window. A valid CO‑SCHED schedule meeting all deadlines corresponds exactly to a feasible preemptive single‑machine schedule. ∎

As before, this hardness motivates restricting to a well‑structured family of co‑schedules.

