Chapter 9: Static Batching and Inference Deadline Guarantees

9.1 Model

We assume synchronous rounds: E episodes start simultaneously, each generating exactly L tokens (worst‑case; shorter episodes are padded). Inference forward passes take time T_{\text{inf}} per token per batch. Episodes are grouped into micro‑batches of size at most B_{\max}, limited by memory. The inference phase has a time budget D_{\text{phase}}.

9.2 Feasibility Theorem

Lemma 9.1 (Feasibility of Static Batching). A round‑robin batching schedule that forms \lceil E/B_{\max}\rceil micro‑batches and processes L token steps per micro‑batch meets all deadlines if and only if

\lceil E/B_{\max}\rceil \cdot L \cdot T_{\text{inf}} \le D_{\text{phase}}.

Proof. Total inference time is the number of micro‑batches times L \cdot T_{\text{inf}}. If this does not exceed D_{\text{phase}}, all episodes finish by the deadline. Conversely, if all deadlines are met, the serial execution of all token steps requires at least \lceil E/B_{\max}\rceil \cdot L \cdot T_{\text{inf}} time, establishing necessity. ∎

9.3 Embedding in the Pipeline

Inference forward passes are treated as additional compute nodes on each pipeline stage, shorter than training micro‑batches (T_{\text{inf}} < T_{\text{train}}). They will be placed in the idle intervals of the training pipeline.

