Chapter 10: Co‑Mem — Joint Memory Planning

10.1 Dual Memory Requirements

Training requires activation memory; inference requires KV‑caches for each active episode. Under a static round‑based schedule where all inference precedes all training, their live intervals are temporally disjoint.

Lemma 10.1 (Temporal Disjointness). Under the AOP‑RL schedule, KV‑caches are live only during [0, T_{\text{inf}}\cdot L]; training activations are live only during [T_{\text{inf}}\cdot L, T_{\text{round}}].

Proof. By construction, inference phase precedes training phase. ∎

Consequently, peak memory is \max(M_{\text{KV}}, M_{\text{train}}), not their sum.

10.2 Co‑Mem Algorithm and Optimality

Within the inference phase, KV‑caches grow monotonically. We can offload caches of completed episodes to slow memory during idle intervals at the end of the inference phase. Because all episodes have equal length, the choice of which cache to offload is irrelevant; any eviction policy that reduces the total active KV‑cache size to fit the memory budget is optimal.

Within the training phase, TopoMem (Chapter 5) applies unchanged. Theorem 10.2 (Co‑Mem optimality). Under temporal disjointness, the two phases can be planned independently, and the resulting memory plan achieves minimal peak memory for each phase, hence overall minimal peak memory.

