Chapter 11: AOP‑RL — Zero‑Bubble Co‑Scheduling

11.1 Bubble Capacity Revisited

The AOP schedule (Chapter 6) provides idle intervals—the startup and cooldown—that can be filled with inference tokens. The total idle time on stage s is (s-1)\tau (startup) plus (P-s)\tau (cooldown), totalling (P-1)\tau time units. To execute an inference wave, all P stages must process the tokens in pipeline. One wave of K tokens takes (P-1)T_{\text{inf}} + (K-1)T_{\text{inf}} = (P+K-2)T_{\text{inf}} time, and must finish before the first training operation starts (for the startup wave) or after all training ends (for the cooldown wave). Both waves provide identical capacity.

Lemma 11.1 (Inference wave capacity). The maximum number of tokens that can be accommodated in a single inference wave (startup or cooldown) is

K_{\max} = \left\lfloor \frac{(P-1)T_{\text{train}}}{T_{\text{inf}}} - (P-2) \right\rfloor.

For large P, this is approximately (P-1)T_{\text{train}}/T_{\text{inf}}.

11.2 AOP‑RL Schedule

We assume each episode fits entirely within one wave (L \le K_{\max}). The schedule processes half the episodes in the startup wave and the other half in the cooldown wave, or all in one if capacity permits. The priority rule during training remains B_I > F > B_W; inference tokens are scheduled only during the provably idle intervals of each stage—specifically, during startup and cooldown, where no training operation is ready.

Theorem 11.2 (AOP‑RL Optimality). Under the condition E\cdot L \le 2 K_{\max} (or tighter, the sum of capacities of two waves), AOP‑RL achieves:

· Zero training bubble (makespan 3M_{\text{train}} + P - 1),
· All inference tokens generated within the specified deadlines,
· Inference completely hidden in training pipeline bubbles.

Proof. The two inference waves are scheduled in the startup and cooldown idle intervals, each respecting the capacity bound. The priority rule ensures that training operations are never delayed by inference, so the training schedule is identical to the pure AOP schedule. The deadlines are met because each episode finishes within its assigned wave, whose duration is bounded by (P-1)T_{\text{train}}. ∎

When the inference load exceeds the bubble capacity, one may reduce M_{\text{train}} to create additional idle time, increasing the round makespan but still meeting deadlines; this defines a latency–throughput Pareto frontier.

