Chapter 13: From Theory to a Static Compiler

The formal framework is designed for direct implementation. The compiler ingests a model specification, an RL environment description, and cluster topology. It constructs the forward BG‑IR, applies Stencil‑AD, builds the CSDF graph, checks consistency and liveness, computes the static batching and memory plans, runs AOP‑RL, and emits a monolithic C file.

The generated code contains no loops beyond the round repetition; every operation—inference token, forward, backward‑input, backward‑weight, DMA transfer—is an explicit function call in a fixed order. All tensor memory is pre‑allocated as static arrays, and communication is implemented via direct RDMA or GPU‑initiated transfers. The binary runs without any runtime framework, garbage collector, or Python interpreter.

