Chapter 14: Conclusion and Future Work

This dissertation has established a complete mathematical foundation for the static compilation of deep learning pipelines. For pure training, we proved that a combination of static AD, memory planning, and a zero‑bubble schedule can achieve the theoretical performance ceiling. For online RL, we extended the theory to cyclic dataflow, resolving the inherent undecidability by adopting the CSDF model, and showed that training and inference can be co‑scheduled with formal optimality guarantees.

The limitations are clear: homogeneous stages, deterministic operations, fixed episode lengths, and linear pipelines. Future work includes general DAG topologies, dynamic sparsity (MoE), fault tolerance, and numerical precision co‑design. The static compiler blueprint awaits full implementation and empirical validation on real clusters. Nevertheless, the theoretical architecture presented here provides a rigorous blueprint for the next generation of AI training infrastructure—compile once, train optimally, serve reliably.

