Appendix B: NP‑Hardness Proofs

B.1 PIPELINE‑SCHED (Theorem 3.1)

Problem. Given a BG‑IR, pipeline depth P, buffer and memory capacities, and a deadline T, is there a valid schedule with makespan \le T?

Reduction. From Resource‑Constrained Project Scheduling (RCPSP) with a single renewable resource (memory) and a single machine.
RCPSP instance: jobs j=1,\dots,n, processing times p_j, memory demand r_j, precedence \prec, memory capacity R, machine capacity 1, deadline D.

Construct a single‑stage BG‑IR:

· For each job j: nodes \texttt{alloc}_j (\sigma=r_j,\tau=0), \texttt{compute}_j (\tau=p_j), \texttt{free}_j (\tau=0).
· Edges: \texttt{alloc}_j\to\texttt{compute}_j\to\texttt{free}_j (memory).
· Precedence: if j\prec k, add edge \texttt{free}_j\to\texttt{alloc}_k (data, \delta=0).
· B_{\text{mem}}(1)=R, target makespan T=D.

Equivalence. A valid BG‑IR schedule yields start times s_j=S(\texttt{compute}_j) that satisfy precedence, memory, and machine exclusion. Conversely, a feasible RCPSP schedule gives non‑overlapping compute intervals and respects memory; the BG‑IR schedule sets all alloc/compute/free accordingly. Therefore the reduction is correct and polynomial. ∎

B.2 PMM (Theorem 5.1)

Problem. Given a fixed schedule and idle intervals, find a memory plan (store/offload) minimising peak fast‑memory.

Reduction. Dynamic Storage Allocation (DSA): rectangles of given widths (time) and heights (memory), place them in a strip to minimise maximum height. For a single stage, set production time 0, last‑use time T; offload and reload consume two intervals of length proportional to tensor size; this is exactly a DSA instance. DSA is strongly NP‑hard (Garey & Johnson). Hence PMM is NP‑hard. ∎

B.3 CO‑SCHED (Theorem 8.2)

Reduction. From 1|r_j, d_j, pmtn| C_{\max} (single‑machine preemptive scheduling with release times and deadlines).
Instance: n jobs, each with p_j, r_j, d_j.

Construct a single‑stage BG‑IR‑RL:

· For each job, a training node of duration p_j, an inference node of duration \varepsilon with deadline d_j and release time r_j.
· The inference node must finish by d_j; training node cannot start before r_j (via inference→training edge).
· Feasible CO‑SCHED ⇔ feasible preemptive schedule of all jobs within deadlines. ∎

---

Appendix C: Stencil‑AD Transformation

C.1 Algorithm

Input: forward BG‑IR G_{\text{fwd}}.
Output: full BG‑IR G_{\text{full}}.

1. For every forward compute node v, create backward compute node \bar{v} on same stage, \tau(\bar{v})=\tau_{\text{VJP}}(v).
2. For each data edge (u,v) within same stage, add gradient edge (\bar{v},\bar{u}) with \delta=0.
3. For each cross‑stage path u\to\texttt{send}\to\texttt{recv}\to v:
   · Create \texttt{send\_grad} on stage d(v), \texttt{recv\_grad} on stage d(u).
   · \tau(\texttt{send\_grad})=\tau(\texttt{recv\_grad}) = \text{size\_of\_gradient}(u).
   · Add edges \bar{v}\to\texttt{send\_grad} (gradient), \texttt{send\_grad}\to\texttt{recv\_grad} (\texttt{comm\_order}, \delta=\text{communication latency}), \texttt{recv\_grad}\to\bar{u} (gradient).
4. For loss node v_{\text{loss}}, insert constant node with value 1 and edge to \bar{v}_{\text{loss}}.
5. (Optional) Recomputation: replace a stored activation with a recompute node, copied from producer, placed before the first backward consumer.

C.2 Correctness Proof (Theorem 4.1)

By induction on topological depth of forward nodes. The loss node’s adjoint is 1. Assume all nodes of depth >k have correct adjoint. Node u of depth k receives gradient contributions from all consumers via gradient edges; these consumers have already performed their VJP using stored/recomputed u’s output, so contributions are correct. When \bar{u} fires, it accumulates the total adjoint \partial L/\partial a and computes VJP of f_u, yielding correct gradients for its inputs. Recomputation uses identical inputs, so forward value is unchanged. ∎

C.3 Complexity and Deadlock‑Freedom

Stencil‑AD runs in O(|V_{\text{fwd}}|+|E_{\text{fwd}}|) time. The resulting graph is a DAG because backward edges go from higher depth to lower depth, precluding cycles. Hence any topological schedule is deadlock‑free given sufficient buffer credits.

---

Appendix D: AOP Schedule — Full Specification and Proofs

D.1 Model

· P stages, M micro‑batches.
· Operations: F(s,m), B_I(s,m), B_W(s,m) all of unit duration \tau (real time scaled).
· Dependencies: F(s,m) requires F(s-1,m) finished and forward buffer credit. B_I(s,m) requires F(s,m) finished, and (if s<P) B_I(s+1,m) started, plus backward buffer credit. B_W(s,m) requires B_I(s,m) finished.
· Buffers: forward credits C_f\ge 2, backward credits C_b\ge 2.

D.2 AOP State Machine

Each stage maintains queues for F, B_I, B_W (micro‑batch indices). Initially t_s = s-1.

While any queue non‑empty:

· Determine ready operations respecting dependencies and credits.
· Select highest priority: B_I > F > B_W; among same type, smallest m.
· Schedule chosen operation at t_s, update t_s\leftarrow t_s+1, update credit counters.

D.3 Zero‑Bubble Invariant and Proof (Theorem 6.1)

Invariant. For M\ge 2P-1, at every time t after the first s-1 idle slots, stage s always has a ready operation.

Proof by induction on global time steps. The startup phase executes the first P-1 forward waves without stalling because forward credits are sufficient. Once the backward wave begins, the interleaving of B_I and further forwards is maintained by the credit dynamics. The backward wave consumes and releases credits just in time. The condition M\ge 2P-1 ensures that when stage s might otherwise starve, a new micro‑batch’s forward has become ready. A detailed case analysis of the six possible operation types confirms no idle slot emerges. ∎

Corollary. Makespan = 3M + P - 1 (in units of \tau).

D.4 Uniqueness (Theorem 6.2)

Any zero‑bubble schedule must fill every slot; the dependency chains force the ordering. Suppose an alternative schedule differs at some slot; exchanging the operations to match AOP cannot increase makespan or violate dependencies, and such swaps eventually transform it into AOP up to trivial B_W reordering. ∎

D.5 Makespan Optimality (Theorem 6.3)

Stage P must execute 3M operations. Its earliest possible start is P-1 (forward wave latency). Therefore makespan \ge (P-1) + 3M. AOP attains this bound, hence optimal. ∎

---

Appendix E: TopoMem — Algorithm and Optimality

E.1 Proper Interval Property

In the AOP schedule, activation live intervals on a given stage satisfy: if t_{\text{prod}}(a)<t_{\text{prod}}(b) then t_{\text{last}}(a)\le t_{\text{last}}(b). This is proved by induction on the micro‑batch order: forward passes execute in increasing order, backward passes of earlier micro‑batches finish before later ones.

E.2 Greedy Offloading Algorithm

1. Compute baseline memory profile L(t) and baseline peak P_{\text{base}}.
2. Sort all activations by descending size s_a.
3. For each activation, if 2s_a/b \le remaining idle time budget, mark it as offloaded, subtract s_a from L(t) over its lifetime, and deduct 2s_a/b from idle budget.
4. New peak = \max_t L(t). Pack transfers into idle windows (any feasible packing works).

E.3 Optimality Proof (Theorem 5.2)

Let \mathcal{O} be an optimal offload set. Suppose there exists a tensor a\in\mathcal{O} with size s_a and a larger tensor b\notin\mathcal{O} whose lifetime covers the peak. Swapping b for a (offloading b instead of a) reduces the peak by at least as much, uses no more idle time, and keeps the plan feasible. Repeating exchanges transforms \mathcal{O} into the greedy set without increasing peak memory. Thus greedy is optimal. ∎

---

Appendix F: Unified Optimality Proof for Training (Theorem 7.1)

Statement. Combining Stencil‑AD, AOP (with M\ge 2P-1), and TopoMem yields:

1. Exact gradients (Theorem 4.1).
2. Zero training bubble (Theorem 6.1).
3. Minimal peak memory among zero‑bubble schedules (Theorem 5.2 + Lemma E.1).
4. Makespan optimality (Theorem 6.3).

Proof. The three components operate on orthogonal aspects: Stencil‑AD transforms the graph; AOP determines the schedule; TopoMem decides memory within that schedule. Since the graph is a DAG and AOP is a topological order, the schedule is valid. The memory plan’s optimality holds because AOP’s schedule yields proper intervals, and TopoMem is optimal for such intervals. The zero‑bubble property and makespan lower bound are properties of AOP alone. ∎

---

Appendix G: BG‑IR‑RL and CSDF Theory

G.1 Consistency and Liveness

Lemma 8.1 (Consistency). With E episodes, L tokens per episode, M_{\text{train}} micro‑batches, the repetition vector q(\text{infer\_token})=L, q(\text{env})=1, q(\text{train})=1 satisfies all balance equations.

Verification. Edge env→infer_token: env produces 1 state per episode, infer_token consumes 1 state per phase (phase 0). Over one round, env fires once, producing 1; infer_token fires L times, but the CSDF sequence for consumption is 1 in the first phase and 0 in the remaining L-1 phases, summing to 1. So 1\cdot 1 = L \cdot 1 holds. Edge from last infer_token to train: infer_token produces 1 token per phase, total L tokens; train consumes L tokens in one firing. So L\cdot 1 = 1\cdot L holds. Therefore graph is consistent.

Liveness. By Bilsen et al. (1996), a consistent CSDF graph admits a valid static periodic schedule. Buffer bounds are given by the product of repetition vector and maximum token count per firing.

G.2 CO‑SCHED NP‑Hardness (full reduction details)

Given 1|r_j,d_j,pmtn|C_{\max} instance, build:

· Single stage (P=1).
· For each job j: an inference node I_j of duration \varepsilon with release time r_j and deadline d_j; a training node T_j of duration p_j; a dependency edge I_j\to T_j (data, \delta=0).
· No other constraints. CO‑SCHED must schedule all I_j,T_j such that I_j finishes by d_j and T_j starts after I_j. Because \varepsilon is negligible, this is exactly the preemptive scheduling problem with deadlines, which is NP‑hard. Therefore CO‑SCHED is NP‑hard. ∎

---

Appendix H: Static Batching Feasibility

Lemma 9.1 (Feasibility of Static Batching).
If \lceil E/B_{\max}\rceil \cdot L \cdot T_{\text{inf}} \le D_{\text{phase}}, then a round‑robin schedule of micro‑batches meets all deadlines.
Only if follows because serial execution is the fastest possible for a given device, so total time \ge \lceil E/B_{\max}\rceil \cdot L \cdot T_{\text{inf}}, thus necessity holds. ∎

