Appendix I: Co‑Mem – Joint Memory Planner

I.1 Temporal Disjointness Lemma (10.1)

Under the round structure (inference first, then training), any KV‑cache for an episode is allocated at the start of its first token and freed after its last token, both within [0, T_{\text{inf}}\cdot L]. Training activations are allocated during the training phase (which begins at T_{\text{inf}}\cdot L), and freed before the round ends. Hence the two classes of live intervals do not overlap. ∎

I.2 Co‑Mem Optimality (Theorem 10.2)

Because the phases are temporally disjoint, peak memory is the maximum of the two independent peaks. Within the inference phase, all KV‑caches have equal size per episode (since L is fixed). Offloading any episode’s cache to slow memory during the idle interval at the end of inference reduces the peak; the greedy choice (offload just enough to fit the memory budget) clearly achieves minimal peak because all caches are identical. Within the training phase, TopoMem provides optimal activation memory. Hence joint optimality follows. ∎

---

Appendix J: AOP‑RL — Detailed Construction and Proofs

J.1 Bubble Capacity Derivation

Idle time on stage s: startup [0, (s-1)\tau), cooldown (T_{\text{total}}-(P-s)\tau, T_{\text{total}}]. Total idle per stage = (P-1)\tau.

A forward inference wave of K tokens requires time on stage s: start at (s-1)T_{\text{inf}}, end at (P-1)T_{\text{inf}} + (K-1)T_{\text{inf}} for the last token on stage P. To fit in the startup bubble, we need (P+K-2)T_{\text{inf}} \le (P-1)T_{\text{train}}. Solving yields K \le \frac{(P-1)T_{\text{train}}}{T_{\text{inf}}} - (P-2). The cooldown wave, running after training, similarly can accommodate the same number of tokens. So total token capacity per round is 2K_{\max} (if episodes are placed in separate waves). The condition in Theorem 11.2 simplifies to E\cdot L \le 2K_{\max} (or using both waves).

J.2 Priority Rule

During idle intervals (startup/cooldown), only inference tokens are ready because no training operation has its dependencies satisfied yet. During the training phase, the priority B_I > F > B_W governs, ensuring zero bubble. Inference is never scheduled when a training operation is ready, thus training is never delayed.

J.3 Optimality Proof (Theorem 11.2)

Since inference uses only idle slots that are unavoidable in any pipeline schedule (the startup and cooldown bubbles), it adds zero extra time to the training makespan. All inference deadlines are met because each episode’s tokens are generated within a wave whose length is bounded by (P-1)T_{\text{train}}, which is set as D_{\text{phase}}. Therefore the co‑schedule is optimal. ∎

---

Appendix K: Unified Co‑Optimality Proof (Theorem 12.1)

Assumptions: consistent BG‑IR‑RL, E\cdot L \le 2K_{\max}, M_{\text{train}}\ge 2P-1, buffer credits \ge 2, memory sufficient for Co‑Mem plan.

1. Correctness: Stencil‑AD applied to the training subgraph yields correct gradients (Theorem 4.1). The CSDF graph is live by Lemma 8.1, so infinite execution is possible.
2. Zero training bubble: The training phase follows AOP exactly because inference tokens are confined to idle slots, so Theorem 6.1 applies.
3. Inference SLOs: By Theorem 11.2, all inference tokens are generated within D_{\text{phase}}.
4. Memory optimality: Co‑Mem provides independent optimal plans for each disjoint phase (Theorem 10.2). Since phases don’t overlap, overall peak memory is minimal.

Thus the schedule satisfies all claims. ∎

