Appendix L: Analysis of Earlier Drafts and Evolution of the Theory

This appendix documents the critical corrections and refinements applied across the two dissertation drafts. The iterative process, guided by advisor feedback, transformed an ambitious but mathematically incomplete proposal into a fully rigorous theory.

L.1 First Draft (Training‑Only)

Original proposal: A static training stack in C, outperforming JAX by >10× via AOP, Stencil‑AD, TopoMem.
Gaps identified:

· NP‑hardness reduction for PIPELINE‑SCHED was incomplete (machine exclusion not properly modelled).
· PMM NP‑hardness only cited DSA without explicit reduction.
· AOP zero‑bubble proof was a sketch, not self‑contained.
· Memory optimality assumed proper intervals without proof.
· 10× speedup claim lacked quantitative decomposition.

Corrections applied in second draft:

· Tightened PIPELINE‑SCHED reduction using RCPSP with a unary machine resource.
· Provided explicit DSA reduction for PMM.
· Expanded AOP proof with inductive invariant and credit management.
· Proved the proper interval property for AOP schedules.
· Replaced the 10× claim with a more rigorous MFU analysis, showing a 1.28–2.54× improvement plus memory‑enabled scaling.

L.2 Second Draft (Training+RL Co‑Scheduling)

New challenges: Cyclic graphs introduced undecidability of liveness. CO‑SCHED NP‑hardness reduction was vague. AOP‑RL bubble condition erroneously doubled idle capacity, and the priority rule conflicted with training zero‑bubble. Memory intervals for KV‑cache and activations were incorrectly assumed proper and compatible. Related work was outdated.

Systematic fixes (final version):

· Cyclic liveness: Adopted CSDF formalism (Bilsen et al., 1996). Proved consistency of BG‑IR‑RL, ensuring polynomial‑time schedulability and liveness guarantees.
· CO‑SCHED NP‑hardness: Constructed a precise reduction from 1|r_j,d_j,pmtn|C_{\max}, citing correct references (Du & Leung, 1993; Chen & Yuan, 2021).
· AOP‑RL bubble capacity: Correctly derived that the total idle time per stage is (P-1) units, not 2(P-1). Recognised that inference wave capacity per bubble is \approx (P-1)T_{\text{train}}/T_{\text{inf}}; two waves double this, but the per‑stage idle sum already accounts for both when pipeline parallelism is considered. The corrected condition remains E L T_{\text{inf}} \le (P-1)T_{\text{train}} if using both waves (details in Appendix J).
· Priority rule conflict: Resolved by restricting inference to strictly idle intervals where no training operation is ready, preserving the B_I > F > B_W priority.
· Memory disjointness: Replaced erroneous joint‑interval properness with a stronger temporal disjointness lemma, based on the round structure, which trivialises joint optimality.
· Related work: Updated to 2024–2025 systems (verl, RLHFuse, ReaL, StreamRL, Braverman et al. KV scheduling), positioning this work as the first static approach with formal guarantees.
· Overall coherence: Added a gap‑analysis table (Chapter 8 of the second draft) and wove the corrections into the final dissertation’s narrative.

L.3 Final Dissertation

The final text integrates all corrections into a single, unified theory. The training part (Chapters 3–7) stands on a rigorous NP‑hardness, proper intervals, and full AOP proofs. The RL part (Chapters 8–13) rests on the CSDF foundation with proven liveness, corrected bubble arithmetic, and a clear memory model. The appendices here provide every missing proof detail, ensuring the dissertation is mathematically self‑contained.

