Appendix N: Production‑Grade Compiler Architecture and Implementation Details

This appendix presents a concrete, production‑oriented design of the static compilation framework described in the dissertation. While the proof‑of‑concept code in Appendix M demonstrates the essential ideas, the present design adds the robustness, modularity, and extensibility required for realistic deep learning workloads. The compiler is written in C++ (C++17) and emits pure C that can be compiled to a standalone binary. We first detail the improved internal architecture, then outline a step‑by‑step roadmap for completing the system.

---

N.1 Compiler Structure Overview

The compiler is organised as a pipeline of well‑defined passes, each corresponding to one of the theoretical components:

```
Front‑end (parse model description)  
    → BG‑IR construction  
    → Stencil‑AD (static AD pass)  
    → AOP Scheduler  
    → TopoMem memory planner  
    → C code emitter  
```

Each pass operates on a shared intermediate representation that evolves from forward BG‑IR to full BG‑IR with backward nodes, to a scheduled and memory‑mapped graph. The design follows a visitor pattern for graph traversal and a pass‑manager architecture for extensibility.

---

N.2 Core Data Structures

N.2.1 Tensor and Type System

All tensors are described by a shape and a data type, enabling size calculations for communication volume and memory planning.

```cpp
enum class DataType : uint8_t { FLOAT32, FLOAT16, BFLOAT16 };
struct Shape { std::vector<size_t> dims; };
size_t tensor_bytes(const Shape& s, DataType dt);
```

N.2.2 Nodes and Edges

The BG‑IR graph is composed of polymorphic node classes derived from a common base class BGNode. This design allows the compiler to easily distinguish between compute, communication, and memory operations, and to attach stage‑specific attributes.

```cpp
class BGNode {
public:
    NodeId id;
    StageId stage;    // 0‑based pipeline stage
    NodeKind kind;    // Compute, Send, Recv, Alloc, Free
    virtual void accept(Visitor&) = 0;
    virtual std::string emit_c_call(const Schedule&, const MemoryPlan&) const = 0;
    virtual ~BGNode() = default;
};

class ComputeNode : public BGNode {
public:
    OpType op;                // e.g., MatMul, LayerNorm
    Shape input_shape, output_shape;
    Duration fwd_time;        // measured or modelled
    Duration bwd_time;        // time for VJP
    // ...
};

class CommNode : public BGNode {
public:
    TensorVolume volume;      // bytes
    Direction dir;            // Send or Recv
    // ...
};

class AllocNode : public BGNode { /* size, lifetime hints */ };
class FreeNode : public BGNode {};
```

Edges are stored separately for efficiency:

```cpp
struct Edge {
    EdgeId id;
    NodeId src, dst;
    EdgeKind kind;   // Data, Gradient, Memory, CommOrder
    Duration latency; // minimum delay for comm edges
};
```

The graph itself maintains node and edge vectors and supports standard operations such as topological sort, reverse post‑order, and substitution of subgraphs (needed for recomputation).

```cpp
class Graph {
public:
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<BGNode>> nodes;
    std::vector<Edge> edges;
    // adjacency lists built on demand
    // ...
    void add_node(std::unique_ptr<BGNode>);
    void add_edge(Edge);
    std::vector<BGNode*> topological_order() const;
};
```

---

N.3 Pass Implementations

N.3.1 Stencil‑AD

The AD pass transforms a forward graph into a forward‑backward graph by creating backward compute nodes and gradient communication. A registry of elementary operations maps each forward op to its vector‑Jacobian product (VJP) implementation.

```cpp
class StencilADPass {
public:
    void run(Graph& g);
private:
    void create_backward_nodes(Graph& g);
    void wire_gradient_edges(Graph& g);
    void insert_gradient_communication(Graph& g);
    void handle_recomputation(Graph& g, const RecomputationPlan& plan);
};
```

The pass traverses forward compute nodes in reverse topological order, building the corresponding backward nodes. For each cross‑stage data edge, it inserts SendGrad and RecvGrad nodes with exact communication volumes. Recomputation is performed by cloning a compute node and redirecting gradient consumers to the clone; this is implemented as a graph‑rewriting step that preserves acyclicity.

N.3.2 AOP Scheduler

The scheduler is a discrete‑event simulation that respects dependencies, buffer credits, and device mutual exclusion. It outputs a Schedule object containing a vector of timestamped operations per stage.

```cpp
class AOPScheduler {
public:
    Schedule compute(const Graph& g, size_t P, size_t M,
                     const CreditConfig& credits);
private:
    bool is_ready(NodeId, const StageState&, const CreditTracker&);
    std::optional<Op> choose_next(StageState&, const CreditTracker&);
};
```

The priority rule B_I > F > B_W is enforced by examining ready queues. The scheduler also verifies the zero‑bubble condition M \ge 2P-1 and, if violated, falls back to a minimal‑bubble schedule. The resulting Schedule provides, for each operation, the exact start time and the assigned stage. This information drives memory planning.

N.3.3 TopoMem Memory Planner

TopoMem takes the fixed schedule and computes an activation memory plan that minimizes peak HBM usage. It first extracts the live intervals of all forward activations (proper intervals under AOP) and the idle intervals of each stage.

```cpp
class TopoMemPlanner {
public:
    MemoryPlan plan(const Schedule& sched, const MemoryBudget& budget);
private:
    LiveIntervals compute_liveness(const Schedule&);
    IdleIntervals compute_idle(const Schedule&);
    MemoryPlan greedy_offload(LiveIntervals&, IdleIntervals&, MemoryBudget);
};
```

The planner sorts tensors by size and greedily offloads the largest that fit within the available idle time, as proven optimal for proper intervals. The resulting MemoryPlan maps each activation to a slot (reuse index) or to an offload decision.

N.3.4 Code Emitter

The emitter produces a single C file containing one function per device. All loops over micro‑batches are completely unrolled; all memory is statically allocated. The design ensures zero runtime dispatch.

```cpp
class CCodeEmitter {
public:
    std::string emit(const Graph& g, const Schedule& sched,
                     const MemoryPlan& mem, const ArchSpec& arch);
private:
    void emit_header(std::ostringstream&);
    void emit_static_buffers(std::ostringstream&, const MemoryPlan&);
    void emit_unrolled_loop(std::ostringstream&, const Schedule&);
};
```

For an RL co‑scheduling extension, the emitter also inserts launch_infer_token calls in the idle slots, following the AOP‑RL bubble placement.

